00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.980
And we definitely
appreciate your willingness

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:30.870
to jump in in that way.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:31.860
And I think that's part of the,

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:32.870
the beauty of it too.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:35.280
Because as you hinted that we
don't always have these types

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:36.780
of interesting conversations.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:38.810
Even though they definitely
influence one another.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:40.723
So I'm hoping that's
something we can really unpack

00:10:40.723 --> 00:10:43.500
in the conversation today
and in this series in general

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.040
as we do that with some of
the other topics as well.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.670
But just to follow up on that, Andra,

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.040
if you could just give us
a little bit of a taste for

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:52.780
from your lens of what you see as our,

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:56.640
in a way our current
political health as a society.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.330
what does that look like at the moment?

00:10:58.330 --> 00:10:59.380
Where are we sitting?

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:04.600
- I wish I could be
optimistic and I am optimistic

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.460
because I do think that there is

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:07.880
the potential for reform

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.740
and the potential for
reconciliation and the often.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:13.680
But if I had to look at the
current state of America

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.410
I have to acknowledge there are problems

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:16.770
and there are serious problems.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:20.190
They didn't change before
or after January 6th

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:23.530
they didn't change before
or after January 20th.

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.630
And I think it's important
for us to realize

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.560
that America is just as sick

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.010
and just as divided

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.340
now with the new administration
as it was previously.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.940
And it's gonna be really important

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:36.663
for us to spend the time.

00:11:36.663 --> 00:11:38.430
I know people want to move past things

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.890
and sometimes they want to do
that for political reasons,

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.250
but it's important for us to diagnose

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:47.280
and address the problems that
brought us to January 6th

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:49.330
that brought us to the last four years,

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:50.970
because I think that,
that's more of a symptom

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.480
than a cause of anything in order

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.180
to address those issues and move forward.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:58.340
So, all of the problems in
terms of partisan polarization

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.880
all the problems in
terms of racial division,

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.740
all of the problems in terms
of people jerking for position

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:06.880
and putting power and
sometimes party ahead

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.510
of what's good for the country,

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:09.935
all of the structural problems

00:12:09.935 --> 00:12:12.950
that were laid bare by
what we've seen happen

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:16.400
in the last few years are still there.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:17.233
And we could ignore it

00:12:17.233 --> 00:12:19.040
and then kick the can down the road

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:21.640
and have another eruption in a few years

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:25.290
or we can try to address these
problems once and for all

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:27.290
and put us in a better position

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:28.580
so that we're more democratic.

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:30.045
So that, we're more just.

00:12:30.045 --> 00:12:32.270
And what I see right now is a tension

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.840
between people who I think have the will

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:36.263
and the willingness to,

00:12:36.263 --> 00:12:38.600
have those hard conversations

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.290
and people who don't want to
have those hard conversations.

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.370
And I think we need to recognize is

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:45.470
that those groups are actually more evenly

00:12:47.184 --> 00:12:48.784
matched numerically than as some

00:12:49.786 --> 00:12:50.619
of us perhaps would want them to be.

00:12:50.619 --> 00:12:51.670
And so we're gonna have
to figure out a way to

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.946
bring people along to have that,

00:12:53.946 --> 00:12:55.970
to have that moment where

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:57.037
they're actually willing to say that.

00:12:57.037 --> 00:12:58.123
And so, I wish we could say

00:12:58.123 --> 00:13:00.720
that we hit rock bottom on January 6th.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.190
I'm not sure that that
is in fact, the case.

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:05.040
- Wow, there's a lot there.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:07.350
And I appreciate you
just really digging in

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.830
and point out, you know,
the reality of these things.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:11.820
I mean, these are more symptoms

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:13.506
than the core issue and the importance

00:13:13.506 --> 00:13:16.410
for us to really be able to
dig in and address those.

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:17.640
And we're definitely
gonna come back to talk

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:19.370
about some of the things
that we need to do,

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:21.680
to be able to effectively work through it

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:23.900
and then move forward
and not just move on.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:25.720
I thought why you brought
up some of those points.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:28.860
I want to pivot to Jeff and just ask you,

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.170
in the midst of everything
that Andra just brought up

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:34.110
how does that impact us as a
society when we're thinking

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.430
about our emotional wellness
and our mental health?

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:38.863
What's the relationship between those?

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.940
- Well, I think certainly

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.080
with what Andra's pointed out

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:48.316
the political instability,

00:13:48.316 --> 00:13:50.040
she didn't use these words,

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.210
but the continued racism

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:53.390
and white supremacy

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:55.240
and how it has been divisive

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:57.790
for our nation from the very beginning,

00:13:57.790 --> 00:13:59.150
right up until now.

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:00.740
I think more than anything else

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:02.960
this has been an affirmation for us

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:05.936
that we as black and brown people

00:14:05.936 --> 00:14:08.500
and those of us who are diverse

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:12.950
liberal whites, and so
on that we're not crazy.

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:14.610
We weren't making this up.

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:17.070
We weren't acting as victims.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:19.210
This is something that is structural

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:21.910
and has been built into the foundation

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:25.230
of the United States and
many parts of the world.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:28.170
And so that affirmation,
and I think for me

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:32.600
as a black person has lent
me a little bit more sanity

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.490
because it is the truth we are seeing.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:38.700
What is part of what is going on

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:41.080
part of the process here and what it is

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.695
that we need to continue to address

00:14:43.695 --> 00:14:46.610
and to try to fix.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.010
But that being said

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:52.670
I've never seen so much
traumatization retraumatization

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:54.420
of black and brown people

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:58.380
by watching the images on television

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:02.760
and other media sources with
the capital insurrection

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:07.250
the way the individuals
were a very narcissistic,

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:11.783
acted out in a very unstable
ways were very determined,

00:15:12.683 --> 00:15:14.420
in the way that they were attacked.

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:16.660
Now that we're finding out of course,

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:19.226
that there may have been
some coordination with

00:15:19.226 --> 00:15:22.306
others within certain places.

00:15:22.306 --> 00:15:26.440
I think all of those
things not only remind us

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:28.900
and are an affirmation, which in some ways

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:30.320
as I said is healthy

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:33.360
that now we know what we're seeing is real

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:38.780
but it also is a constant trauma

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:41.450
that we're seeing the
way that people act out

00:15:42.585 --> 00:15:45.280
the way they're trying to stop us

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:48.500
from voting the continued oppression.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:51.740
All of those things, I
think really contribute

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:54.180
to our post-traumatic stress disorder,

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:56.290
continue to contribute to our

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:58.690
post-traumatic slavery disorder

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:01.840
but continue to keep us
emotionally unstable.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:05.230
And why now, we as mental
health professionals

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:07.520
have to address these issues in therapy

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:10.160
and really look at
positive mental attitude

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.233
spirituality, empowerment, strategies,

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:17.050
strategies working within
the political system

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:20.293
and every other system
to address these issues.

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:22.630
- Yeah, so important.

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:23.830
So important on so many fronts.

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:26.170
So you both brought out so
many important topics as well.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:28.170
I think one thing that runs through

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:29.970
both of your comments
and something that a lot

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:32.470
of us talk about is that
these things aren't new.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:35.370
So in some ways, sometimes
it's feels to some

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:37.370
like it's a new conversation,
but it really isn't.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:40.000
I mean, these things have
been embedded in ongoing

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:40.833
and in a sense,

00:16:40.833 --> 00:16:43.560
I do see some ways that
people are listening

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.610
with more of an ear for
empathy than they have

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:47.040
in the past,

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.850
at times where people
might have brushed it off

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.721
or just assume that those were
things that were happening

00:16:51.721 --> 00:16:52.600
out there that weren't
really happening right here

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:53.433
where they were or weren't.

00:16:53.433 --> 00:16:54.960
They didn't realize that these were things

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.660
that were happening to people they knew.

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.280
So even for me, as I've shared my story

00:16:58.280 --> 00:16:59.850
that it's people feel shocked

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:00.870
that someone that they work with,

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:01.703
interact with,

00:17:01.703 --> 00:17:03.230
is having these same
experiences in their minds.

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.200
Somehow they just put
that out there as other.

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:07.890
So, I think there is a
better understanding.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:10.447
There's still a lot in the long
way to go in terms of that,

00:17:10.447 --> 00:17:12.580
but really reconciling the fact

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:15.260
that these aren't fringe
episodes or fringe

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:16.093
things that are happening,

00:17:16.093 --> 00:17:17.480
this is baked and it's happening

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:19.590
across the board in so
many different ways.

00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:20.423
And then Jeff,

00:17:20.423 --> 00:17:22.356
you talked about just all the
ramifications of that as well.

00:17:22.356 --> 00:17:26.040
And so, as a mental health professional

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:27.470
and you start to talk
about this a little bit

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:28.630
in terms of some of the practice

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:31.670
but how do we deal with
the state that we're at

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:34.690
and really terms in terms of
trying to really address it?

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.020
And this ties into what Andra was talking

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:37.500
about in terms of the
political piece as well.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:38.770
These aren't things that we can ignore

00:17:38.770 --> 00:17:40.195
but we really have to dig in and say

00:17:40.195 --> 00:17:41.390
we do need to address it.

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:42.443
So where do we start?

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:45.945
- Well, let me say this
to your prior point

00:17:45.945 --> 00:17:48.783
what a difference a pandemic makes?

00:17:48.783 --> 00:17:51.170
Because it is the pandemic

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:53.440
and everything that came along with it.

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:55.740
Of course, first we didn't understand

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:57.270
but now you can connect the dots.

00:17:57.270 --> 00:18:00.255
We've never been through a
pandemic in our particular,

00:18:00.255 --> 00:18:02.330
since the last a hundred years.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:04.270
So, now we're seeing
how everything connects

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:08.500
but it is the pandemic itself
that really opened up the eyes

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:12.330
and the hearts of many
provided them the empathy to

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:15.820
understand what social
injustice looks like,

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:16.930
and what it feels like

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:19.280
and what people have been
going through forever,

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:21.140
because they were feeling the pain too.

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:23.540
This was a universal pain that actually

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:27.170
changed the trajectory of the world.

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:29.630
Absolutely extraordinary.

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:32.550
And so what we need to do

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:36.170
I believe in, and I'm
sure many people agree is

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:39.490
addressing the mental health
tsunami that came after

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:42.830
as part of this COVID-19 pandemic

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:46.420
that we are going through
can continue to go through.

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:50.316
And so that means now more than ever

00:18:50.316 --> 00:18:52.860
not only addressing mental illness,

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:55.350
but the importance of mental health

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:58.910
as being part of our everyday lives.

00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:00.193
It's not just about,

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:04.580
as you know I love to exercise every day

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:07.710
but we also now have
to exercise our minds.

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:10.320
We have to exercise our spirituality.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:11.780
We have to be able to connect

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:14.680
with other people and talk about the pain

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:16.770
our informal support groups,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:18.530
our formal support groups,

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:20.905
our therapy was seeing more people,

00:19:20.905 --> 00:19:24.100
including people of
color coming into therapy

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:25.390
than ever before.

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:28.290
As a matter of fact, when we
look at the general population

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:32.320
a poll showed that one in six Americans

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:37.660
in 2020 actually accessed
mental health treatment.

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:39.610
And that is incredible.

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:42.800
In some ways it's very sad
that it had to happen that way.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:45.220
In some ways it's very
encouraging in that,

00:19:45.220 --> 00:19:47.350
finally we're addressing the importance

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:49.110
of being strong emotionally.

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:51.970
So those are the things we
have to keep pushing forward

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:54.908
and smashing that stigma of
talking about mental illness

00:19:54.908 --> 00:19:58.210
in the black and brown
and other communities

00:19:58.210 --> 00:20:01.820
and continuing to address
that every day actively.

00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:03.474
- So well said, and in so many levels.

00:20:03.474 --> 00:20:05.410
And yeah, as you mentioned

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:07.010
that it's encouraging,

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:08.540
disappointing and
encouraging at the same time,

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:10.020
but I think there's a lot that we can take

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:10.853
from that to say, okay,

00:20:10.853 --> 00:20:12.410
well, look, we are starting to address it

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:13.930
and we're not just moving on.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:15.070
And yes, we have made progress

00:20:15.070 --> 00:20:17.120
in terms of decreasing
stigma, but you're right.

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:18.190
We still have to really smash

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:20.680
that ethos and get past that to really get

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:23.130
to a place where we can have healing.

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:24.360
And at the same token, Andra,

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.150
I was just curious to hear your thoughts

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:27.567
'cause you've talked about this

00:20:27.567 --> 00:20:28.910
or you hinted at this already

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:30.595
and talked about it elsewhere.

00:20:30.595 --> 00:20:31.850
What do we need to do to move forward

00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:34.160
in terms of also equally importantly

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.040
addressing some of
these political tensions

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:39.100
that our city and that we've seen,

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:40.550
we've already seen when we ignore them

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:43.420
they just bubble up and more
than bubble up, they explode.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:44.620
So what do we do to,

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:45.840
I almost wanna say prevent

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.243
that from continuing to happen as a cycle?

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:53.970
- That's really complicated and complex

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.610
because in the political arena,

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:58.630
people have to contend with the fact

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:02.000
that some people who actually
have helped to foment

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.260
the division are empowered.

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:06.127
And are likely to stay
in power structurally

00:21:06.127 --> 00:21:08.450
because they're coming from districts

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:09.283
or they're coming

00:21:09.283 --> 00:21:13.600
from States where people have
a diametrically opposed view

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:17.216
to the situation where you
have people who perhaps

00:21:17.216 --> 00:21:18.970
believe that Donald Trump really

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:20.623
had the election stolen from him.

00:21:23.007 --> 00:21:25.560
Or people who actually
see themselves pitted

00:21:26.724 --> 00:21:28.930
against folks of the
opposing political party

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:31.900
and something that to
them would be existential

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:34.820
and equivalent to like
a partisan death match.

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:38.330
And so we have to figure
out as individuals

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:41.210
and then we actually have to
hold our leaders accountable

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:44.370
for figuring out ways to
lower the temperature.

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:46.620
And that's really difficult
in this moment when

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:49.993
not everybody is actually
willing to work together.

00:21:49.993 --> 00:21:52.895
So actually it was just
teaching this in my class.

00:21:52.895 --> 00:21:54.470
We've seen increasing evidence

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:56.710
of what we call negative partisanship.

00:21:56.710 --> 00:21:58.010
So it was a concept that was coined

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:00.980
by one of my colleagues and
his graduate students at Emory.

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:03.320
And the idea of negative
partisanship is not

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.140
that I met political party per se

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:06.630
because of what I believe in,

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:09.630
but because I framed
it in a way that says,

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:11.773
I know I don't believe
what those other folks say.

00:22:13.564 --> 00:22:15.030
And so while that can be clarifying

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:17.757
it also has the potential to other people.

00:22:17.757 --> 00:22:19.500
And that has serious consequences when

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:21.990
we see people being othered,

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:24.130
taken to sort of,

00:22:24.130 --> 00:22:25.270
it's in right.

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:26.730
That's when genocide happens, right?

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:29.230
Because if you think about historically

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:30.800
the ways that things have happened

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:32.630
like that's justified people feeling

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:35.250
that it's okay to inflict
violence against people

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:37.320
and to try to eliminate people.

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:38.810
And we've seen that in our rhetoric,

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:40.570
where you've seen social media posts,

00:22:40.570 --> 00:22:42.820
where people think it's perfectly okay

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:45.270
to talk about executing
their political opponents.

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:46.860
I'm like, we shouldn't
be talking about that

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:49.760
in the United States that
runs counter to our ideals

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:52.670
that we need to acknowledge
historically how

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:54.800
people have been completely unabashed

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:57.480
and using violence when it
was convenient for their ends.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:59.210
And whether that is sort of,

00:22:59.210 --> 00:23:01.323
absolutely holding people up,

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:03.900
whether that's enslavement,

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:05.100
whether that is denying people,

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:06.710
their rights, like there's
just a lot of violence

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:08.960
in American history that
goes unacknowledged.

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:13.340
We have to say, no, stop.

00:23:13.340 --> 00:23:14.600
That's unacceptable.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:18.209
And I think even has to say
that this is unacceptable,

00:23:18.209 --> 00:23:22.010
we rebuke and sometimes we've
removed people from office

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:24.023
even when we agree with them,

00:23:25.388 --> 00:23:27.160
even when politically it's advantageous

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:28.250
to keep these folks in office

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.520
because they're gonna
vote the way that you want

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:31.750
on certain types of issues.

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:34.240
And that because of negative partisanship

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:36.130
because of polarization
is the unwillingness

00:23:36.130 --> 00:23:37.876
that I've seen people do.

00:23:37.876 --> 00:23:42.050
It's how you can have
somebody say, you know what,

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:43.863
I think Donald Trump caused a riot,

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.480
but I'm not gonna vote to like a victim

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:49.950
in an impeachment trial.

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:52.880
And if he runs for president
again, you know what?

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:54.080
I'm gonna support him, right?

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:56.410
Because we got the same
letter behind our name.

00:23:56.410 --> 00:23:58.130
And I think people who are Democrats

00:23:58.130 --> 00:23:59.570
also need to acknowledge

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:02.940
that there are people who would
use the same twisted logic

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:06.260
for a member of their own
party, if it met their end.

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:07.093
Right now I'm not trying

00:24:07.093 --> 00:24:08.519
to create a false equivalency.

00:24:08.519 --> 00:24:11.250
Because we saw a lot of that
in the last couple of weeks.

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:13.630
And I haven't seen
Democrats actually like,

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:16.050
try to start a riot recently right now.

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:19.110
But in theory, there could be
some crazy person who decides

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:20.330
that that is the way to go.

00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:22.060
And Democrats would
then be on the hot seat

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:24.710
and have the responsibility
to stand up against that.

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:27.640
I think that we just need
some courageous people

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:30.220
more of them who are
actually willing to stand

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:31.520
up and speak truth to power,

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:35.240
wherever it is and actually
be willing to take the risk

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:38.200
of short-term political
loss of their careers.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:40.250
I haven't seen that happen.

00:24:40.250 --> 00:24:42.030
And so as voters

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:45.120
and we need to take that
voting responsibility seriously

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:47.590
and not just turn out in
record breaking numbers

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:49.690
because the country caught on fire.

00:24:49.690 --> 00:24:51.700
And in some cases almost
literally when we think

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:53.170
about sort of,

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:54.580
the environmental disasters

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:55.940
that have happened in the West,

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:58.390
but that, look different
on the East coast where,

00:24:58.390 --> 00:24:59.440
when we see flooding.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:02.430
But like the country was literally on fire

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:04.490
because of COVID or because of wildfires

00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:07.570
or because of demands for racial justice.

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:10.160
And all of a sudden everybody
realized what the stakes are.

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:12.000
Those stakes are important
when you're voting

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:12.880
for dogcatcher,

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:14.470
when you're voting for city council,

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:16.820
when you're voting for
your state legislatures.

00:25:17.908 --> 00:25:21.320
And sometimes some of these people come

00:25:21.320 --> 00:25:23.868
into sort of like their
public service careers

00:25:23.868 --> 00:25:25.590
via some of these other routes.

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:27.040
And if you wanna stop that

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:28.340
and say that we don't stand

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:30.530
for that type of uncivil behavior.

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:32.499
We don't stand for people
who wanna be revisionist

00:25:32.499 --> 00:25:37.197
in their history and deny
racism and deny marginalization.

00:25:37.197 --> 00:25:39.627
You got to stop them wherever,

00:25:39.627 --> 00:25:41.970
whatever level they're running for office,

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:44.510
even if they held office
for a long period of time.

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:48.200
And maybe even in the past
they've done nice things for you.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:49.250
- Wow. There's a lot there.

00:25:49.250 --> 00:25:51.079
So many things we could respond to.

00:25:51.079 --> 00:25:54.210
I think you've pulled out again
so many important concepts.

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:55.470
And I think this piece about

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:56.990
whether it's reactionary versus

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:59.120
whether we're in this for the long haul

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:01.390
and really having some proactive measures

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:02.330
but I'm also curious

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:03.620
'cause you've painted a picture in terms

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:07.090
of what needs to happen
at a leadership level.

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:07.923
I'm curious,

00:26:07.923 --> 00:26:09.380
what do you feel like
is the responsibility?

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:11.930
And you mentioned this as well,
but on the individual level

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:14.790
versus the large scale
leadership political level.

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:17.630
And if there's any
intersection between those two?

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:18.840
- I think that there's
a lot of intersection.

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:19.840
I think a lot of people think

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:20.940
that polarization is this thing

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:24.010
that people in Washington DC do.

00:26:24.010 --> 00:26:25.280
Or that people may be doing

00:26:25.280 --> 00:26:27.180
in their respective state capitals.

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:30.420
Now, those leaders come from the masses

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.860
and those leaders are getting
their cues from the masses.

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:35.250
And one of the things
that's actually been really

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:37.360
interesting is that, we always talk about

00:26:37.360 --> 00:26:39.460
in person and in crisis of the past,

00:26:39.460 --> 00:26:41.071
you heard about the difference

00:26:41.071 --> 00:26:42.640
between trustee and
delegate representation

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:44.159
when we elect people.

00:26:44.159 --> 00:26:46.890
Are they there to just
kind of put their finger up

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:48.733
in the wind and then just
vote the way that they think

00:26:48.733 --> 00:26:51.480
that people in their
constituency want them to vote

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:53.710
or are they there to be
able to make tough decisions

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:55.430
maybe technical decisions that are based

00:26:55.430 --> 00:26:57.070
on expertise and learning

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.610
and a lot of politicians

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:00.550
kind of use some
combination of both kind of

00:27:00.550 --> 00:27:03.080
depending on which issue is at hand.

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:06.700
In the last month it's been
funny to see people go up

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:09.460
to their leaders and hold
them accountable if you will

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:10.293
because they were like,

00:27:10.293 --> 00:27:11.430
we didn't send you there to think,

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:13.730
we sent you there to do our bidding.

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:14.670
And it was like, yeah, well,

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:16.871
sometimes true leadership means

00:27:16.871 --> 00:27:19.163
that you stand up and say, you know what?

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:24.641
your personal opinion is based in a lie.

00:27:24.641 --> 00:27:27.332
So, it's my responsibility
to show you that that's a lie

00:27:27.332 --> 00:27:30.210
and then help bring you
to a different place.

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:32.060
But that also suggests that

00:27:32.060 --> 00:27:34.990
there are people who can be easily led

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:39.160
into a type of mob mentality
where they're not thinking.

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:41.680
And so we have to guard ourselves

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:43.970
against sort of becoming part of the mob.

00:27:43.970 --> 00:27:46.700
So part of that is making sure

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:49.710
that in terms of your
own media consumption

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:51.920
that you are guarding your heart.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:54.470
So it's really nice to
go into your echo chamber

00:27:54.470 --> 00:27:56.290
whatever echo chamber that is,

00:27:56.290 --> 00:27:58.490
and only read and consume the stuff

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:00.559
that already confirms what you think.

00:28:00.559 --> 00:28:02.980
Sometimes that stuff isn't true.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:05.270
And there are some ways
that you can think about it.

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:06.460
So like the tools that I give

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:08.020
to my students are,

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:09.511
watching cable news,

00:28:09.511 --> 00:28:13.850
prime time gets really
opinionated certain time of day,

00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:15.130
eight to 11,

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:17.321
nine to 11 in particular, right?

00:28:17.321 --> 00:28:19.180
Those folks may be journalists.

00:28:19.180 --> 00:28:20.230
They may not be journalist.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:22.390
So you should know the
background of your TV posts

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:24.250
and know sort of like
when they're actually

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:26.130
like spouting off of their opinion

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:28.540
versus when they're delivering hard news.

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:30.320
You should know when
they have round tables

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:32.470
kind of like, who are the journalists,

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:34.460
who are the political hats,

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:35.921
who were there,

00:28:35.921 --> 00:28:38.810
who's there to be objective to
provide outside information.

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:40.700
Also you should vet sources.

00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:43.671
So if one news outlet
is reporting something

00:28:43.671 --> 00:28:47.520
and other credible
establishment news outlets

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:48.950
are not reporting it,

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:51.140
you might be suspect they
could be on the Vanguard

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:52.800
and other folks could catch up eventually.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:57.800
But that is a clue that one
news source is picking it up

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:01.700
and other people aren't that
there, this may not be true.

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:06.370
So I would, not necessarily
forward that on Facebook,

00:29:06.370 --> 00:29:09.370
if you can't verify the
source of what's going on.

00:29:09.370 --> 00:29:12.670
And then, there are other
places that are trusted

00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:15.900
where these people have no
idea what they're doing.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:17.350
These people don't have any training.

00:29:17.350 --> 00:29:18.850
They don't have accountability.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:20.720
They don't have any editors.

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:22.810
You shouldn't be
listening to that as well.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:26.060
And I wouldn't trust any
news source that doesn't have

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:27.270
an ombuds person,

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:29.990
and that doesn't have a
history of retractions, right?

00:29:29.990 --> 00:29:31.800
Because all journalists make mistakes.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.170
And the difference between
journalism and propaganda

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:36.320
is that journalists will correct mistakes.

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:37.870
Propagandists don't.

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:41.330
And so you have to understand what it is

00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:42.630
that you're listening to.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:45.449
And it's okay sometimes to cut it off

00:29:45.449 --> 00:29:48.830
even though news is on 24 hours a day

00:29:48.830 --> 00:29:50.790
you don't have to watch it 24 hours a day.

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:54.100
You should probably go
spend time with your family.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:56.660
And the real news will still
be there when you get there.

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.750
- Yeah, that's really good.

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:00.290
You're, touching on some
of the mental health too.

00:30:00.290 --> 00:30:01.940
He already put in some of those practices

00:30:01.940 --> 00:30:02.810
you made your disclaimer

00:30:02.810 --> 00:30:04.840
about not being a mental health expert.

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:06.430
But I think you've got some good tools

00:30:06.430 --> 00:30:08.450
that you're sharing with
our listeners already,

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:09.283
but I think,

00:30:09.283 --> 00:30:11.290
everything you've mentioned is so spot on.

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:12.460
Jeff, I was curious with everything

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:14.377
that Andra has mentioned.

00:30:14.377 --> 00:30:16.950
How you advise people as
she's been talking about

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:18.220
in their media consumption

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.460
both in terms of how much to take in,

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:22.650
but then also in terms of
what she's talking about,

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:23.840
about discernment?

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:26.220
And how how do we react
to some of those things?

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:28.670
Because some of the things
that are happening are real

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:30.430
and we've touched on
that it affects people

00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:32.329
but how do you guide people in terms of

00:30:32.329 --> 00:30:34.390
those responses to
everything that's happening

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:35.801
in politics and in the media?

00:30:35.801 --> 00:30:39.630
- Well, Andra was spot on with her advice.

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:41.230
And one of the things that we say

00:30:41.230 --> 00:30:43.420
as mental health experts is

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:46.770
know when you've reached
your saturation level.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.689
When it comes to consuming the news

00:30:49.689 --> 00:30:52.330
be able to get what it is that you want

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:55.460
because breaking news
is the same news that's

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:59.230
being played over and over
and over and over again.

00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:02.400
And so breaking news,
ain't what it used to be.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.990
So you can get that information quickly

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:06.640
and then once you get it,

00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:10.060
then go on to something
else that relaxes you

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:14.361
as you are absolutely
processing that information.

00:31:14.361 --> 00:31:17.130
And as well as she has said,

00:31:17.130 --> 00:31:19.430
again, the importance of getting it

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:21.890
from different news sources,

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:24.490
because certainly you
don't want your information

00:31:24.490 --> 00:31:26.100
to be homogeneous

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:29.070
because then you're not
getting different opinions.

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:30.793
It's not allowing you to think.

00:31:31.841 --> 00:31:33.090
It's not allowing you to process it

00:31:33.090 --> 00:31:34.920
by hearing the different voices

00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:37.570
and giving you a much more information.

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:40.020
But one of the most
important things that I think

00:31:41.121 --> 00:31:42.910
that you should really consider

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:45.550
is that we've lost many friendships.

00:31:45.550 --> 00:31:48.130
We've lost many family connections

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:50.270
because of the partisan politics.

00:31:50.270 --> 00:31:52.210
What it is that we're hearing in the news?

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:55.410
What it is that we have come to believe?

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:58.600
And so it's important
that you stay connected

00:31:58.600 --> 00:32:01.300
to your friends, to your family,

00:32:01.300 --> 00:32:03.280
to people that you may work with,

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:05.960
who not only have the same opinions

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:08.960
but especially who have
differing opinions,

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:10.970
because they're you find common ground

00:32:10.970 --> 00:32:12.270
you try to find common ground,

00:32:12.270 --> 00:32:14.793
you learn something new.

00:32:17.874 --> 00:32:20.160
It's not only the intellectual processes

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:24.180
that increase but also your social IQ,

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:27.452
but more than anything else you want

00:32:27.452 --> 00:32:30.060
to maintain those
connections with other people

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:31.970
because it goes beyond the news.

00:32:31.970 --> 00:32:34.170
It goes to your humanity

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:37.320
and learning how to live in a world,

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:38.810
how to live in a country,

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:41.270
where people have differing opinions

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:43.380
but you can respect their opinions,

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:46.230
especially if they're not based on hate

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:48.610
and racism and prejudice

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:51.260
but are based more than anything

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:54.100
on having different perceptions.

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:55.900
That's what's always made America

00:32:55.900 --> 00:32:57.860
when it has been great, great.

00:32:57.860 --> 00:33:01.140
But that's what makes us as human beings

00:33:03.865 --> 00:33:07.100
the social kinds of people that we are

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:09.810
it's important to
maintain those connections

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.340
especially in times like these.

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:13.720
- Yeah. I think that's so important.

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:15.920
You've both made so many great points.

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:17.290
I think there's so much rich content

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:19.070
that people can really dig into here

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:21.223
and really think about aspects where,

00:33:21.223 --> 00:33:23.510
we've all fallen short.

00:33:23.510 --> 00:33:25.470
We're human, especially
in this time with COVID.

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:27.492
I think one of the main
things that we've had to

00:33:27.492 --> 00:33:29.090
we emphasize for ourself.

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:30.270
I know I've had to reemphasize

00:33:30.270 --> 00:33:32.660
for my students as well
is to give ourselves

00:33:32.660 --> 00:33:34.240
at times to know that
things are more difficult

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:35.370
than they usually are.

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:38.103
And there are gonna be a lot more missteps

00:33:38.103 --> 00:33:39.780
than we're typically accustomed

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:41.230
to taking in the first place.

00:33:41.230 --> 00:33:42.880
But to again have that willingness

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:45.890
to be able to forgive ourselves
and forgive those around us.

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:47.980
I guess I think that gets to
your point, Jeff, as well.

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:50.815
And to add just a point about
really being able to get

00:33:50.815 --> 00:33:54.370
and have community beyond the differences.

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:56.300
Andra, I think you hit
that point very early on

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:57.970
as you were talking about just how

00:33:57.970 --> 00:33:59.590
the differences are so deep seated.

00:33:59.590 --> 00:34:00.500
And you've talked about some

00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:02.920
of the frameworks from
how people are focusing

00:34:02.920 --> 00:34:05.990
on the othering more and more
than they have in the past.

00:34:05.990 --> 00:34:07.570
Not that it wasn't there
in the past in other ways

00:34:07.570 --> 00:34:10.670
but how dangerous that
can be in a lot of ways.

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:12.700
So definitely appreciate
both the points that you

00:34:12.700 --> 00:34:15.020
are pulling out in this conversation.

00:34:15.020 --> 00:34:17.470
As we wrap up, I did
want to just talk briefly

00:34:17.470 --> 00:34:20.250
about some of the additional
steps that we think

00:34:20.250 --> 00:34:23.990
about in communities of color
as well and how we move on.

00:34:23.990 --> 00:34:27.090
So, or how we try to move on
after having moved through.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:28.580
So there was an article
in the New York times,

00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:29.430
a couple of weeks ago,

00:34:29.430 --> 00:34:31.630
specifically from black staffers who were

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:34.690
at the Capitol working on January six.

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:35.590
And they talked a lot

00:34:35.590 --> 00:34:38.900
about their specific
traumatization that happened

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:39.733
in that moment.

00:34:39.733 --> 00:34:43.170
I'm they assumed that of all
places as black individuals

00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:44.003
in this country,

00:34:44.003 --> 00:34:45.730
that they would be safe
in that environment

00:34:45.730 --> 00:34:47.640
and all the fears that they had

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:50.310
fear for losing their
lives in that situation.

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:52.420
And then all of the
stress that they've known,

00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:54.905
they felt after the fact
and even just saying,

00:34:54.905 --> 00:34:57.940
sometimes if we haven't gone through it

00:34:57.940 --> 00:34:59.350
the post-traumatic stress can feel

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:02.230
like it's an intellectual
thing that we're talking about.

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:03.790
Once you go through a situation like that

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:05.720
then you realize, okay, this is real.

00:35:05.720 --> 00:35:08.120
And so I'm curious from both of you

00:35:08.120 --> 00:35:10.880
how is as for black individuals,

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:12.817
all the ramifications of that moment

00:35:12.817 --> 00:35:15.210
and how we process that moving forward

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:17.330
both from a political framework

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:20.468
and also from our mental health framework.

00:35:20.468 --> 00:35:23.551
- So this might sound a little weird,

00:35:25.131 --> 00:35:26.760
but in some ways I think

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:30.000
that there were affirming moments

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:32.880
as tragic as this was,

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:37.769
I think it is important to
acknowledge that for many folks,

00:35:37.769 --> 00:35:39.780
this wasn't a surprise.

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:44.073
Like we knew that white
supremacy has sort of,

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:48.150
violent goals and that they
could manifest themselves.

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:51.030
And I don't think anybody
woke up per se on January 6th

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:53.160
other than the people who were planning

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:55.370
that riot and expected that to happen.

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:57.130
But on the other hand,

00:35:57.130 --> 00:35:58.690
people who are on with the knowledge

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:01.031
that white supremacy is violent

00:36:01.031 --> 00:36:03.050
weren't shocked by what they saw.

00:36:03.050 --> 00:36:05.250
Even though as citizens in the moment,

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:06.510
you can be shocked and horrified

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:08.820
but what it is that you're seeing.

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:13.164
And so I used the term affirming to say

00:36:13.164 --> 00:36:16.540
that it affirms what people knew deep down

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:18.793
in their core about racism.

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:23.590
So that you're not shocked
or surprised by what you saw.

00:36:23.590 --> 00:36:27.370
And it also counters
those who wanted to say

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:28.930
that America isn't racist

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:30.630
or that America was past race,

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:32.460
we took care of all of that in the 1960s.

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:33.990
Shortly after Barack Obama

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:38.457
there is no such thing as
racism in America anymore.

00:36:38.457 --> 00:36:41.113
Donald Trump was doing mapping off.

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:45.830
We knew that, that was made bare.

00:36:45.830 --> 00:36:49.030
And there's a strange comfort in knowing

00:36:50.488 --> 00:36:53.570
that you weren't crazy that
your experience is real

00:36:53.570 --> 00:36:57.060
and that it is tied to the
experience of other people.

00:36:57.060 --> 00:36:58.250
And the other thing that I think is really

00:36:58.250 --> 00:37:00.600
interesting is sort of
this idea of community.

00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:03.489
And I think that there are
mental health applications

00:37:03.489 --> 00:37:04.540
for this in political science.

00:37:04.540 --> 00:37:07.060
We always talk about group consciousness

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:09.220
within the African-American community.

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:10.330
We talk about a political

00:37:10.330 --> 00:37:12.420
psychological concept called linked fate

00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:14.480
which makes it highly predictive

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:16.590
of African-American political behavior.

00:37:16.590 --> 00:37:18.550
And in terms of policy preferences

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:20.708
in terms of voting behavior
and other kinds of things.

00:37:20.708 --> 00:37:23.240
It's that thing that makes
black people look alike

00:37:23.240 --> 00:37:24.160
even though there's a lot

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:26.690
of heterogeneity in black communities.

00:37:26.690 --> 00:37:29.040
But part of the idea of
believing that what happens

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:31.250
to other black people affects you

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:34.040
and recognizing that you're
not just an individual,

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:37.610
but that politically because
of the social marker of race

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:39.140
and the social stigma of race,

00:37:39.140 --> 00:37:41.350
that your identity is salient

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:43.700
and that it makes sense
for people to join together

00:37:43.700 --> 00:37:46.130
to try to collectively
improve their community

00:37:46.130 --> 00:37:48.770
and their status within the community.

00:37:48.770 --> 00:37:51.510
That also creates
opportunities for healing,

00:37:51.510 --> 00:37:54.110
because you don't have
to suffer this alone.

00:37:54.110 --> 00:37:56.360
Other people saw the exact
same thing that you do,

00:37:56.360 --> 00:37:57.410
that you did.

00:37:57.410 --> 00:37:58.763
And if you talk about it,

00:38:00.010 --> 00:38:02.560
that's a way to help work through some of

00:38:02.560 --> 00:38:04.260
that trauma together because

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:06.820
part of the trauma is isolation.

00:38:06.820 --> 00:38:09.510
Is feeling that you're the only one

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:11.260
and you weren't.

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:13.540
There were lots of people who saw that

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:15.253
and they weren't always all black,

00:38:16.410 --> 00:38:17.243
who saw that.

00:38:17.243 --> 00:38:18.550
But in particular with respect

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:20.440
to the African-American community,

00:38:20.440 --> 00:38:23.000
yeah, I would say
probably the vast majority

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:24.560
of the 44 million

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:26.910
of us in the United
States kind of saw that

00:38:26.910 --> 00:38:29.470
and understood what was going on.

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:33.000
And there's a way for us
to be able to work together

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:34.500
and combat that.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:36.706
And I think you, there's
some lessons like,

00:38:36.706 --> 00:38:41.090
post-traumatic trauma and
stress have been around.

00:38:41.090 --> 00:38:42.070
They may not have been named

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:43.410
as such in previous generations

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:45.110
but we have a name for it now,

00:38:45.110 --> 00:38:47.090
and we can talk through it.

00:38:47.090 --> 00:38:49.530
And sometimes I think that
there are important lessons here

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:51.970
for how we talk about bad things,

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:54.530
and I will leave it to Jeff to talk

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:56.740
about when it's appropriate to
bring this up with children.

00:38:56.740 --> 00:38:58.250
But I think perhaps some of the shock

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:00.590
particularly for people who are younger is

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:02.750
that they thought America
was past all of this

00:39:02.750 --> 00:39:04.690
in the last six years,

00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:07.783
seven to eight years have shown us that,

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:10.700
we're really not past all of this.

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:11.910
But I actually

00:39:13.499 --> 00:39:15.746
as troubling and as dissatisfied

00:39:15.746 --> 00:39:17.220
and as frustrated as I can be,

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:20.100
that I can look back at what happened

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:21.530
in the fifties and what was happening

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:23.660
in the 19 teens and twenties
and what was happening

00:39:23.660 --> 00:39:27.453
in the 1880s and 1890s sort
of shows this common thread.

00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:30.360
There's the frustration
of why are we still kind

00:39:30.360 --> 00:39:32.560
of treading the same
ground that we had before

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:33.830
but there's also a comfort

00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:37.270
in knowing other people
saw this, name this

00:39:37.270 --> 00:39:38.600
tried to combat it,

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:40.460
and that we have an opportunity

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:42.620
to kind of push the ball forward

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:44.680
farther than they were able to

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:46.210
because of the tools and the freedoms

00:39:46.210 --> 00:39:48.370
that we have at our disposal.

00:39:48.370 --> 00:39:49.203
And I think

00:39:49.203 --> 00:39:53.690
it's better to have intergenerational
discussions about this

00:39:54.650 --> 00:39:57.340
so that people don't understand that,

00:39:57.340 --> 00:39:59.137
this is the first time
we're experiencing this.

00:39:59.137 --> 00:40:01.950
And sometimes our elders
have tools that they

00:40:01.950 --> 00:40:03.470
haven't always imparted to us

00:40:03.470 --> 00:40:05.460
because they were trying
not to traumatize us.

00:40:05.460 --> 00:40:07.149
And that wasn't a healthy way

00:40:07.149 --> 00:40:07.982
for them to deal with their trauma.

00:40:07.982 --> 00:40:09.940
And it's not a healthy way to help us deal

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:10.980
with our trauma today.

00:40:10.980 --> 00:40:12.780
So I see opportunities.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:14.100
And so that's why I'm
calling it affirming.

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:15.320
Not that I think that what happened

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:16.570
on January six is a good thing

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:18.600
because of course that was horrible.

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:19.683
- Well, that's so good.

00:40:19.683 --> 00:40:20.516
- I agree.

00:40:20.516 --> 00:40:21.349
I agree.

00:40:22.750 --> 00:40:25.750
And specifically going to this question

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:29.120
about black journalists and
what they've been through

00:40:29.120 --> 00:40:33.280
we know that they've been
traumatized and retraumatized.

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:36.980
They are having some real issues dealing

00:40:36.980 --> 00:40:39.130
with the dichotomy

00:40:39.130 --> 00:40:43.020
of reporting the news
and telling the truth

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:45.960
but telling it in a way
that doesn't get them

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.760
in trouble with the parent organization

00:40:48.760 --> 00:40:51.830
where there may be people who
are extremely conservative

00:40:51.830 --> 00:40:53.910
who may own those news outlets

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:57.078
or getting people really angry

00:40:57.078 --> 00:40:59.760
the general public, their readers,

00:40:59.760 --> 00:41:01.340
the people who watch them

00:41:01.340 --> 00:41:03.790
by talking about what's
really in their hearts.

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:07.180
So they have to keep it
extremely professional

00:41:07.180 --> 00:41:12.180
and just report it in a way
that reporting is usually done

00:41:12.780 --> 00:41:14.963
not putting your emotions in it,

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:18.700
especially if you just
giving straight news.

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:20.280
So they're facing the wrath

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:23.300
of some people in that way who are saying

00:41:23.300 --> 00:41:26.070
we don't want to know about
your black experience.

00:41:26.070 --> 00:41:28.610
They're facing the wrath
of people who are saying

00:41:28.610 --> 00:41:33.450
why aren't you telling us more
about your black experience?

00:41:33.450 --> 00:41:35.720
And then we know that traditionally

00:41:39.235 --> 00:41:41.010
that black journalists,
black broadcasters,

00:41:41.010 --> 00:41:41.893
and so on,

00:41:43.969 --> 00:41:46.913
there's been systematic
institutional sorts of racism

00:41:48.020 --> 00:41:50.640
prejudice in the places that they work

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:52.990
where they have been treated traditionally

00:41:52.990 --> 00:41:55.110
as second and third class citizens

00:41:56.444 --> 00:41:58.870
not being given their due feeling

00:41:58.870 --> 00:42:01.810
like they are an outside group.

00:42:01.810 --> 00:42:03.330
I know this because I've done a lot

00:42:03.330 --> 00:42:05.290
of the work with these journalists.

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:08.470
And so traditionally there's been a PTSD

00:42:08.470 --> 00:42:10.660
as Andra has talked about.

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:14.060
They've experienced it
in all of these avenues

00:42:14.060 --> 00:42:17.530
but especially with what's
going on in the world right now

00:42:17.530 --> 00:42:21.600
around COVID and around,
political oppression

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:25.730
and social injustice.

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:28.390
But at the same time, we also know

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:30.507
and this is life in COVID worlds.

00:42:30.507 --> 00:42:33.570
We also know that it's important

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:38.220
for them to be able to hook
up with affinity groups

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:41.250
so that they can talk
about some of their issues

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:45.300
and as well be able to
connect with one another

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:50.060
with regard to informal support
groups within their networks

00:42:50.060 --> 00:42:53.083
and also getting that therapy too.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:55.780
- Yeah. So I'll start by both of you,

00:42:55.780 --> 00:42:57.380
and yes that's definitely a COVID life

00:42:57.380 --> 00:42:59.477
as you've demonstrated for us very well.

00:42:59.477 --> 00:43:02.518
- Did you see the mom at
the, on the floor saying

00:43:02.518 --> 00:43:03.888
come back here.

00:43:03.888 --> 00:43:06.295
- In my mind, I envisioned that.

00:43:06.295 --> 00:43:08.390
(Addy laughing)

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:09.440
so many important points.

00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:10.300
I think you've both

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:13.650
just the affirmation and
the tension that's there.

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.550
And Jeff has even mentioned
with the journalist

00:43:15.550 --> 00:43:17.757
and I think for all of us
in a lot of different ways

00:43:17.757 --> 00:43:20.170
and I think that's part of the challenge

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:22.660
for us really moving
forward is to be able to

00:43:22.660 --> 00:43:24.150
to lean into that,

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:25.560
to both of those aspects

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:27.580
and not ignore either one of those.

00:43:27.580 --> 00:43:28.810
Andra, I love the point that

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:32.410
you mentioned about the
elders bringing in aspects

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:34.670
that they can help us
with through as well.

00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:37.090
Whereas some of those things
might not have been spoken

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:39.990
about before for fear of retraumatization,

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:42.320
but that as we're all
going through it already,

00:43:42.320 --> 00:43:44.900
the time is definitely appropriate.

00:43:44.900 --> 00:43:47.010
There's so much more that
we could have jumped into.

00:43:47.010 --> 00:43:48.190
I definitely appreciate both

00:43:48.190 --> 00:43:50.790
of you being here and for the listener

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:53.390
this is obviously an ongoing
conversation we'll be having

00:43:53.390 --> 00:43:56.690
from different angles on
the Addy Hour moving forward

00:43:56.690 --> 00:43:59.070
but I'm deeply appreciative
to both Dr. Jeff Gardere

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:02.500
and Dr. Andra Gillespie for
joining on this first episode

00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:04.920
for really launching in and
giving us a lot to think

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:06.600
about and these are conversations

00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:08.610
that as you'll see,
we'll have some takeaways

00:44:08.610 --> 00:44:09.960
but there's things that
we'll also be able to

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:12.060
continue to think about
throughout the week.

00:44:12.060 --> 00:44:13.630
So again, just deeply appreciative to both

00:44:13.630 --> 00:44:15.280
of you for being willing to jump in

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:17.670
on this first episode for
really setting the tone

00:44:17.670 --> 00:44:19.478
for how things will go moving forward.

00:44:19.478 --> 00:44:21.220
- Thank you.

00:44:21.220 --> 00:44:22.053
- Thank you.

00:44:22.053 --> 00:44:24.427
It was an honor and a pleasure
to be on the Addy Hour

00:44:24.427 --> 00:44:28.140
and to spend the time also with you Andra

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:30.590
you are spot on and everything you say

00:44:30.590 --> 00:44:33.100
and I can to you forever,

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:34.000
but I'll be...

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:35.140
- I appreciate that.

00:44:35.140 --> 00:44:37.200
I have liked you for years

00:44:37.200 --> 00:44:38.510
so, this has been fun.

00:44:38.510 --> 00:44:39.343
- Thank you.

00:44:39.343 --> 00:44:41.558
But I'll be listening
to you on the Addy hour.

00:44:41.558 --> 00:44:43.180
(Andra and Addy laughing)

